she could swim. The world record of 8:06.68, which she held, was felt to have no shelf life, but what was possible for her – 8:04, 8:03, 8:02? The only other question was who would get the other medals."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the prelims Ledecky posted 8:12.86, winning the heat by over 6½ seconds. In the final, if there was any doubt at all of her dominance, it was over by 100 metres, as she led Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/130283"} "Jazz Carlin"] " by almost two seconds. That lead was increased at each split, eventually winning in a world record 8:04.79, besting Carlin by 11.38 seconds, the largest margin in the event since " [:a {:href "/athletes/51124"} "Debbie Meyer"] " won by 11.7 seconds in 1968, the first time it was held at the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Ledecky’s 200-400-800 triple matched the feat of Meyer, who won the same events in 1968. Her four gold medals in Rio had been previously done, among women, by " [:a {:href "/athletes/47465"} "Kornelia Ender"] " in 1976, " [:a {:href "/athletes/51223"} "Amy Van Dyken"] " in 1996, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/126679"} "Missy Franklin"] " in 2012, all trailing the six golds won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/47512"} "Kristin Otto"] " in 1988. Her three individual golds made her the eighth woman to achieve this at one Olympics in swimming, also trailing Otto, who won four in 1988."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/126653"} "Katie Ledecky"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "8:12.86 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "8:04.79 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}